<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">
<!--
  zedx_mount.urdf.xacro - the ZED X stereo camera, parametrised so its pose
  can be chosen at spawn time instead of hard-coded.

  Fixes a real bug in the original sidewalk_robot.urdf.xacro: robot_frames.yaml
  and the ZED SDK both measure/report the camera's pose as the LEFT LENS, not
  the housing centre - the old xacro placed the HOUSING at the given pose and
  then derived the left lens at +baseline/2 in y, so the left lens actually
  landed 60mm away from the pose that was supposedly being set.

  Here the macro's xyz/rpy IS the left lens pose (matching how it's measured
  and how robot_frames.yaml documents it); the housing and right lens are
  derived from it, not the other way around. A pure +/-baseline/2 shift along
  y is valid regardless of pitch, because pitch rotates about the y axis
  itself - the one rotation this mount ever uses (roll and yaw are 0 in every
  design note so far).

  Usage (see sidewalk_sim/urdf/husky_real_zedx.urdf.xacro):
    <xacro:zedx_camera name="zedx_front" parent="zedx_front_camera_center"
                        xyz="-0.010 0.060 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>

  THE EXAMPLE ABOVE CHANGED ON 2026-08-29 AND THE OLD ONE WAS ACTIVELY
  MISLEADING. It read rpy="0 0.087 0" — a ~5 degree pitch that was DESIGN INTENT
  and was never real. The camera's true tilt was measured that day as 0.0565 rad
  (3.24 degrees nose down), and it is applied ONCE, at the mount joint, not here.
  Passing a pitch to this macro would apply it a SECOND time. The old example
  also parented to "oak-d-base-frame", a leftover from a camera this robot does
  not have (verified: no Movidius USB device, zero depthai topics).

  The authoritative pose lives in four files that move together — see
  sidewalk_bringup/config/robot_frames.yaml for the list and the reasoning.

  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  FIELD OF VIEW CHANGED ON 2026-08-29 AND IT SPLITS THE RUN HISTORY IN TWO.
  READ THIS BEFORE COMPARING ANY TWO SIMULATION RUNS.

  Every Gazebo camera in this file carried a horizontal_fov of 1.8151 rad
  = 104.00 degrees, from the first commit until that date. (Written without
  the angle brackets on purpose: xacro copies comments into the expanded
  URDF, and a stray old value in tag form would show up in anyone's grep of
  the output.) The real ZED X this project owns is 73 degrees
  horizontal (SKU ZED-312110, 4.6 mm lens — datasheet, "ZED X Available
  Models"). The simulation was therefore running a camera 42 percent wider
  than the hardware it exists to mirror.

  That is not cosmetic. Field of view decides how much structure lands in
  each frame, and that drives feature count, loop-closure recognition and
  map coverage — the three things every measurement in this project is
  about. At a fixed distance the old camera saw a scene 73 percent wider
  and, the aspect ratio being unchanged, 73 percent taller than the real
  one — roughly THREE TIMES the ground area per frame
  (tan(52°)/tan(36.5°) = 1.7296 per axis; 1.7296² = 2.99).

  CONSEQUENCE: every simulation run recorded BEFORE 2026-08-29 used a
  104-degree camera. Those runs remain comparable WITH EACH OTHER and are
  NOT comparable with anything recorded after this change. This is the same
  situation as the real robot's camera pose, which was wrong in runs 1
  through 6 — a silent change of experimental conditions across a date, not
  a bug that "got fixed".
  Strength of that claim, stated honestly: for every run since the tree
  entered version control on 2026-08-22 it is VERIFIED, because git blame
  shows the value untouched from the first commit to this one. For runs
  older than that there is no version history to check; they used this same
  file, which already carried 1.8151 on the day it was imported.

  WHERE 1.8151 CAME FROM IS UNKNOWN, AND THAT IS THE HONEST ANSWER.
  `git log -S1.8151` returns exactly two commits: 7673646, the initial
  import of an already-written tree, and e8d9bfe, which merely copied the
  same number onto the new stereo pair a few hours later. So the value
  arrived fully formed and version control cannot say from where. It is
  not a datasheet figure for either ZED X lens (4.6 mm is 73°, 2.2 mm is
  110°). It is not the SDF default either (1.047 rad, 60°, which is what
  the two overhead observation cameras in the office worlds use). It is
  also NOT the 80° that sidewalk_perception carried for its own optics
  until 2026-08-29 — that was a third wrong number, unrelated to this one.
  No note anywhere in the tree explains 1.8151. Rather than invent a
  provenance for it, this says plainly that it has none.
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-->

  <!-- Precise datasheet figures from docs/mount/index.html - the long axis
       (163.4mm) runs left-right across the robot (robot Y), matching the
       mount brief and the original xacro. -->
  <xacro:property name="zedx_depth"    value="0.0367"/> <!-- x: front-to-back -->
  <xacro:property name="zedx_length"   value="0.1634"/> <!-- y: long axis -->
  <xacro:property name="zedx_height"   value="0.0318"/> <!-- z -->
  <xacro:property name="zedx_mass"     value="0.240"/>
  <xacro:property name="zedx_baseline" value="0.120"/>

  <!-- stereo:=true swaps the Gazebo sensor from the single depth camera to a
       true two-lens stereo pair (see the long comment below for why that
       matters); noise_stddev sets the per-camera Gaussian image noise in the
       stereo mode, unitless in [0,1] per channel (0.005 ~ 1.3 grey levels;
       realistic range 0.002-0.01 per ENGINEERING_NOTES.md 2.2). Both are inert when
       stereo is false, so existing users are byte-identical. -->
  <xacro:macro name="zedx_camera" params="name parent xyz rpy stereo:=false noise_stddev:=0.005">

    <!-- The anchor: exactly the pose given, on the LEFT lens. -->
    <link name="${name}_left_camera_frame"/>
    <joint name="${parent}_to_${name}_left_camera_frame" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${parent}"/>
      <child link="${name}_left_camera_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="${xyz}" rpy="${rpy}"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="${name}_left_camera_optical_frame"/>
    <joint name="${name}_left_camera_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_left_camera_frame"/>
      <child link="${name}_left_camera_optical_frame"/>
      <!-- REP-103: camera optical frame is Z-forward, X-right, Y-down. -->
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-pi/2} 0 ${-pi/2}"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Right lens: baseline further along -y from the left one. -->
    <link name="${name}_right_camera_frame"/>
    <joint name="${name}_left_to_right_camera" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_left_camera_frame"/>
      <child link="${name}_right_camera_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="0 ${-zedx_baseline} 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="${name}_right_camera_optical_frame"/>
    <joint name="${name}_right_camera_optical_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_right_camera_frame"/>
      <child link="${name}_right_camera_optical_frame"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${-pi/2} 0 ${-pi/2}"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Housing: centred between the two lenses, baseline/2 from the left.
         Visual uses the real Stereolabs mesh (already installed on the
         Jetson via zed-ros-wrapper - already in metres, already correctly
         axis-aligned: Y is the 163.6mm long axis, Z is the 31.8mm height).
         Its own origin sits at the housing's vertical/lateral centre but at
         the BOTTOM face in Z, so it needs a -height/2 shift to match this
         macro's convention of the housing frame being at its geometric
         centre. Collision stays a simple box - cheaper, and precise contact
         geometry doesn't matter here. -->
    <link name="${name}_link">
      <visual>
        <geometry>
          <mesh filename="package://zed_interfaces/meshes/zedx.stl"/>
        </geometry>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${-zedx_height/2}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <material name="zedx_black">
          <color rgba="0.05 0.05 0.05 1.0"/>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <geometry>
          <box size="${zedx_depth} ${zedx_length} ${zedx_height}"/>
        </geometry>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="${zedx_mass}"/>
        <inertia ixx="0.0003" ixy="0" ixz="0"
                 iyy="0.0001" iyz="0"
                 izz="0.0003"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${name}_left_to_housing" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_left_camera_frame"/>
      <child link="${name}_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 ${-zedx_baseline/2} 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="${name}_imu_link"/>
    <joint name="${name}_housing_to_imu" type="fixed">
      <parent link="${name}_link"/>
      <child link="${name}_imu_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Gazebo depth camera, publishing on the real ZED wrapper's topic
         names so sidewalk_slam needs no remapping between sim and hardware.
         min/max depth (1.0 / 35.0 m) matches sidewalk_perception/config/
         zedx_front.yaml, so the sim's blind zone matches the real one.
         Used when stereo=false (the default).

         The 1.0 m near clip below is CORRECT HERE and must stay: on a
         `type="depth"` sensor <near> really is the depth blind zone, so it
         models the real camera's min_depth honestly. Do NOT propagate this
         value to the stereo branch further down - those are plain colour
         cameras where <near> deletes geometry from the IMAGE instead, which
         is a different thing entirely and blinded a whole indoor run. See
         the long comment on the stereo left lens. -->
    <xacro:unless value="${stereo}">
    <gazebo reference="${name}_left_camera_frame">
      <sensor type="depth" name="${name}_camera">
        <update_rate>15</update_rate>
        <camera>
          <!-- 73.00 degrees horizontal = 1.2741 radians. THE FULL STORY,
               including what this replaced and why every run recorded
               before 2026-08-29 is now in a separate comparison class, is
               in the FIELD OF VIEW banner in this file's header. Read it.

               CONVERSION, so nobody has to redo it:
                 73 deg x pi / 180 = 1.27409035 rad, kept to 4 decimal
                 places as 1.2741 rad, which reads back as 73.0006 deg.
               SOURCE: ZED X / ZED X Mini datasheet, "ZED X Available
               Models" table, SKU ZED-312110 = ZED X, 4.6 mm lens, no
               polarizer — the unit this project owns. Field of View:
               Max. 73 deg (H) x 45 deg (V) x 87 deg (D).
               (Previously 1.8151 rad = 104.00 deg, origin unknown.)

               THE VERTICAL ANGLE IS NOT AN INPUT HERE, AND WHAT IT COMES
               OUT AS DOES NOT MATCH THE DATASHEET. SAY SO IN ANY
               SIM-VERSUS-REAL COMPARISON.
               The SDF camera element has exactly one field-of-view knob,
               <horizontal_fov> — there is no <vertical_fov>. [VERIFIED —
               /usr/share/sdformat9/1.6/camera.sdf line 8 is the only "fov"
               element in the file.] Gazebo derives the vertical angle from
               the image aspect ratio:
                 vertical = 2 * atan( tan(horizontal/2) / (width/height) )
               [INFERENCE — that is gazebo-classic's Camera::UpdateFOV; the
               implementation is in gazebo/rendering/Camera.cc, which the
               runtime packages do not ship, so it was not read here. Two
               ways to settle it, neither needing the real camera: read
               Camera.cc in the gazebo-classic source, or start gzserver on
               this model and compare fy in the published camera_info with
               fx — equal fx and fy means the aspect-ratio derivation, and
               fy = 1297.4 px is what this file now predicts.]
               At 1920 x 1200 the aspect ratio is exactly 1.6000, so
               73.00 deg horizontal gives 49.64 deg vertical. The real
               camera is 45 deg vertical. THAT 4.64 DEGREE (10.3 PERCENT)
               EXCESS IS A REAL RESIDUAL AND IT IS LEFT VISIBLE ON PURPOSE.

               Why not just fix it. The datasheet's own three angles are
               not self-consistent for the ideal pinhole lens Gazebo
               renders: 73 and 45 imply an aspect ratio of
               tan(36.5)/tan(22.5) = 1.7864, not the sensor's 1.6000, and
               imply an 80.6 deg diagonal, not the 87 deg printed. That is
               expected — the real lens distorts (the same datasheet gives
               TV Distortion -6.7%) and the angles are stated as maxima on
               the raw image. With square pixels, no single pinhole camera
               can hit all three. One axis has to absorb the error:
                 pin horizontal at 73.00 -> vertical 49.64 (4.64 too wide)
                 pin vertical at 45.00 -> horizontal 67.07 (5.93 too
                     narrow; 45 deg vertical would need 1.170561 rad in)
               Horizontal is pinned, because it is the number Gazebo takes
               as input and the one that governs left-right coverage, which
               is what loop closure lives on.

               THERE IS ONE ESCAPE HATCH AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT USED.
               <camera><lens><intrinsics><fx>/<fy> exist and the spec says
               they OVERRIDE horizontal_fov [VERIFIED — camera.sdf lines
               140/143, "X focal length (in pixels, overrides
               horizontal_fov)"], so an unequal fx/fy could hit 73 and 45
               at once. That would model a sensor with non-square pixels;
               the real ZED X has square 3 um pixels, so it would trade a
               disclosed 4.64 deg error for an undisclosed lie about the
               geometry, and it would change what the plugin writes into
               camera_info. Whether Gazebo Classic 11's plain CameraSensor
               even honours <intrinsics> is [UNVERIFIED] here. If this is
               ever attempted it is a separate experiment with its own
               before/after, not a tweak folded into this one. -->
          <horizontal_fov>1.2741</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>1920</width>
            <height>1200</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>1.0</near>
            <far>35.0</far>
          </clip>
          <!-- NO <noise> BLOCK HERE - and that is deliberate, not an oversight.
               Read this before adding one.

               The simulated camera is noise-free, and that has a real
               consequence for SLAM. Run #4 measured visual odometry reporting
               standard deviations of 0.0001 m - "I know where I am to a tenth of
               a millimetre". RTAB-Map judges each candidate loop closure against
               that confidence, so a sane closure disagreeing by 11 cm scores as
               a 3.5-sigma outlier and is discarded. All 224 loop closures in
               that run were rejected for exactly this reason. The map was still
               accurate (0.141 m ATE) because odometry drifted so little, but the
               loop-closure half of SLAM contributed nothing.

               The obvious fix is to add camera noise here. It does not work.
               Gazebo silently ignores <camera><noise> on a <sensor type="depth">:
               the Gaussian-noise shader lives in the plain CameraSensor render
               path, and DepthCameraSensor bypasses it. This was measured, not
               assumed - with stddev set to 0.5 (nominally +/-127 grey levels,
               enough to obliterate the picture) the delivered temporal noise was
               0.0002 grey levels and the maximum frame-to-frame difference was
               exactly 0. The tag parses and expands into the URDF perfectly; it
               simply has no effect.

               Working alternatives, if honest covariance is needed:
                 1. A small node that subscribes to the image, adds Gaussian
                    noise and republishes. Reliable, but 1920x1200 at 15 Hz is
                    ~100 MB/s of pixel work on a Jetson that is already busy, and
                    it still leaves the DEPTH perfect.
                 2. Split the sensor: a <sensor type="camera"> for RGB, which
                    does honour <noise>, alongside the depth sensor. Costs an
                    extra render pass and changes the topic wiring.
                 3. Raise RGBD/OptimizeMaxError in rtabmap_zedx.yaml so loop
                    closures are not rejected so aggressively. Cheapest, but it
                    tunes around the symptom rather than modelling the sensor.
          -->
        </camera>
        <plugin name="${name}_camera_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_openni_kinect.so">
          <baseline>${zedx_baseline}</baseline>
          <alwaysOn>true</alwaysOn>
          <updateRate>15.0</updateRate>
          <cameraName>${name}/zed_node</cameraName>
          <imageTopicName>rgb/image_rect_color</imageTopicName>
          <cameraInfoTopicName>rgb/camera_info</cameraInfoTopicName>
          <depthImageTopicName>depth/depth_registered</depthImageTopicName>
          <depthImageInfoTopicName>depth/camera_info</depthImageInfoTopicName>
          <pointCloudTopicName>point_cloud/cloud_registered</pointCloudTopicName>
          <frameName>${name}_left_camera_optical_frame</frameName>
          <pointCloudCutoff>1.0</pointCloudCutoff>
          <pointCloudCutoffMax>35.0</pointCloudCutoffMax>
          <distortionK1>0.0</distortionK1>
          <distortionK2>0.0</distortionK2>
          <distortionK3>0.0</distortionK3>
          <distortionT1>0.0</distortionT1>
          <distortionT2>0.0</distortionT2>
          <CxPrime>0.0</CxPrime>
          <Cx>0.0</Cx>
          <Cy>0.0</Cy>
          <focalLength>0.0</focalLength>
          <hackBaseline>0.0</hackBaseline>
        </plugin>
      </sensor>
    </gazebo>
    </xacro:unless>

    <!-- ==================================================================
         STEREO MODE (stereo=true): the Phase-1 fix for the noise problem
         documented at length above - "alternative 2", done properly.

         One <sensor type="multicamera"> rather than two separate camera
         sensors, deviating from ENGINEERING_NOTES.md 5's letter ("two plain cameras")
         while satisfying everything that rule exists to enforce:
           * <noise> is HONOURED - multicamera uses the plain CameraSensor
             render path (ENGINEERING_NOTES.md 2.2 lists it beside type="camera"), the
             one the depth sensor bypasses.
           * both lenses render from a single sensor update, so left/right
             stamps are identical -> exact-sync (approx_sync=false), which
             the SLAM launch's own comments call the stricter, better mode.
           * the plugin writes <hackBaseline> into the RIGHT camera's
             P-matrix only, and stamps BOTH camera_infos with the same left
             optical frame - the two hand-written requirements of the rule,
             enforced by construction.
         The delivered noise and stamp identity are MEASURED at validation
         time, not assumed (the depth-noise trap above was also "obviously
         fine" until measured).

         Topic names match the real zed-ros-wrapper layout
         (left|right/image_rect_color + camera_info under ${name}/zed_node),
         so sim and hardware stay interchangeable and existing consumers
         (system_monitor, ORB-SLAM3 configs) keep working. Gazebo cameras
         are ideal pinhole (D=0, R=I): the images are born rectified, hence
         publishing straight to *_rect names and
         Rtabmap/ImagesAlreadyRectified=true in rtabmap_zedx.yaml.

         There is deliberately NO depth sensor in this mode - depth comes
         from stereo disparity inside RTAB-Map, like the real camera. One
         sensor per mode, one render pipeline to reason about.
         ================================================================== -->
    <xacro:if value="${stereo}">
    <gazebo reference="${name}_left_camera_frame">
      <sensor type="multicamera" name="${name}_stereo_camera">
        <update_rate>15</update_rate>
        <camera name="left">
          <!-- 73.00 deg horizontal = 1.2741 rad (73 x pi / 180 =
               1.27409035, to 4 dp 1.2741 = 73.0006 deg). ZED X datasheet,
               "ZED X Available Models", SKU ZED-312110 (4.6 mm lens, no
               polarizer — our unit): Max. 73 (H) x 45 (V) x 87 (D) deg.
               WAS 1.8151 rad = 104.00 deg until 2026-08-29, which is the
               camera every earlier run in this project was recorded with.
               Full reasoning, the vertical-FOV residual and the run-history
               consequence: the FIELD OF VIEW banner in the header of this
               file, and the long comment on the depth sensor above. Do not
               change this number without reading both.
               Vertical is not an input: the SDF camera element has no
               <vertical_fov>, so Gazebo derives it from the aspect ratio.
               At 1920 x 1200 (1.6000) that gives 49.64 deg vertical
               against the real 45 deg — a known 4.64 deg excess that must
               be disclosed, not tuned out. -->
          <horizontal_fov>1.2741</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>1920</width>
            <height>1200</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <!-- NEAR CLIP: 0.1, NOT 1.0. Read this before "restoring" it.

               These are plain COLOUR cameras. <near> here is a RENDER clip:
               geometry closer than it is not drawn AT ALL. It is NOT the
               depth blind zone - in stereo mode depth is computed from
               disparity inside RTAB-Map, and its floor is set by
               Stereo/MaxDisparity=128 in rtabmap_zedx.yaml
               (focal length in pixels * 0.120 m / 128).

               ***THAT FLOOR MOVED ON 2026-08-29 AND NOTHING WAS RETUNED.***
               This paragraph used to read "750 px * 0.120 / 128 = 0.70 m".
               750 px was never independent - it is what the OLD 104-degree
               field of view implied: (1920/2) / tan(104/2) = 750.1 px.
               Now that the horizontal field of view is the real 73 degrees,
               the simulated focal length is
                 (1920/2) / tan(73/2) = 960 / 0.740027 = 1297.3 px
               and the stereo depth floor becomes
                 1297.3 * 0.120 / 128 = 1.216 m,   not 0.70 m.
               So the simulated stereo blind zone has gone from comfortably
               BELOW the real camera's 1.0 m min_depth to ABOVE it. The sim
               is once again harsher than the hardware up close, which is
               exactly the failure mode the rest of this comment describes.

               Stereo/MaxDisparity IS DELIBERATELY LEFT AT 128 HERE. Field
               of view and disparity range are two variables and this change
               is allowed to move one. To restore a ~1.0 m floor the
               arithmetic is 1297.3 * 0.120 / 1.0 = 155.7, i.e.
               Stereo/MaxDisparity ~= 156 (rounded up, 160 for headroom);
               matching the old 0.70 m floor would need
               1297.3 * 0.120 / 0.70 = 222.4. Neither is applied. Whoever
               takes that on runs it as its own before/after experiment.
               The value lives at rtabmap_zedx.yaml:282, and docs/SOLVED.md
               (the "<near>1.0 on the stereo cameras" entry) still quotes
               the stale 750 px / 0.70 m pair.

               The depth-camera branch above DOES use <near>1.0</near>
               correctly, because there <near> really is the depth blind
               zone. Copying that value here looked consistent but does
               something entirely different, and it cost a whole run.

               MEASURED 2026-08-23 with near=1.0, robot facing a wall head-on:
                 3.00 m -> 981 ORB features      1.20 m -> 454
                 2.00 m -> 772                   1.00 m ->  31  (tracking floor is ~50)
                 1.50 m -> 336                   0.90 m ->   0  (wall gone; contrast 24->7)
               Below 1 m the wall vanished from the image entirely. In a
               13x11 m office the robot is inside that zone constantly, which
               produced 170 odometry resets in one drive (14.1 per 100 m
               against a gate of 2). A real ZED X still SEES a surface at
               0.5 m - it just returns no depth for it - so the 1.0 render
               clip made the simulation harsher than the hardware, indoors
               only. Outdoors nothing is ever that close, which is why
               Phase 1 never caught it. -->
          <clip>
            <near>0.1</near>
            <far>35.0</far>
          </clip>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>${noise_stddev}</stddev>
          </noise>
        </camera>
        <camera name="right">
          <!-- Right lens sits -baseline along the sensor frame's y, matching
               the URDF joint above. The camera child names MUST be literally
               "left"/"right": the plugin keys both the topic suffixes and
               the right-only hackBaseline off them. -->
          <pose>0 ${-zedx_baseline} 0 0 0 0</pose>
          <!-- MUST MATCH THE LEFT LENS EXACTLY — 73.00 deg = 1.2741 rad,
               same datasheet line, same 2026-08-29 change from 1.8151 rad
               (104.00 deg). Two lenses with different fields of view do not
               form a stereo pair: the disparity search would be comparing
               images at different scales. See the left lens comment. -->
          <horizontal_fov>1.2741</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>1920</width>
            <height>1200</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <!-- Must MATCH the left lens exactly (see its comment above).
               A mismatched near clip between the two lenses would silently
               break disparity in the overlap region. -->
          <clip>
            <near>0.1</near>
            <far>35.0</far>
          </clip>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>${noise_stddev}</stddev>
          </noise>
        </camera>
        <plugin name="${name}_stereo_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_multicamera.so">
          <alwaysOn>true</alwaysOn>
          <updateRate>0.0</updateRate>
          <cameraName>${name}/zed_node</cameraName>
          <imageTopicName>image_rect_color</imageTopicName>
          <cameraInfoTopicName>camera_info</cameraInfoTopicName>
          <frameName>${name}_left_camera_optical_frame</frameName>
          <hackBaseline>${zedx_baseline}</hackBaseline>
          <distortionK1>0.0</distortionK1>
          <distortionK2>0.0</distortionK2>
          <distortionK3>0.0</distortionK3>
          <distortionT1>0.0</distortionT1>
          <distortionT2>0.0</distortionT2>
        </plugin>
      </sensor>
    </gazebo>
    </xacro:if>

  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
